how do you register event stream objects in cataloon vercel json-render
To register event stream objects in Vercel's json-render framework, you map component event bindings to runtime handlers using the defineRegistry utility. Instead of dealing with raw stream manipulation on the client, the streaming parser (createJsonRenderTransform) progressively builds the UI spec while user actions are automatically routed through declared callback hooks. 

GitHub
 +3
1. Define the Catalog Events
First, specify the allowed events and props for your component using a Zod schema passed to defineCatalog. 

json-render
 +1
typescript
import { defineCatalog } from '@json-render/core';
import { z } from 'zod';

export const myCatalog = defineCatalog({
  components: {
    StreamButton: {
      description: 'A button that dispatches click actions',
      props: {
        label: z.string(),
        streamId: z.string()
      }
    }
  }
});
Use code with caution.
2. Map and Register Handlers via defineRegistry
Use defineRegistry to wire the component implementation and bind specific execution behaviors to its emitted actions. The component receives an emit or on helper function to dispatch occurrences upstream. 

GitHub
typescript
import { defineRegistry } from '@json-render/react';
import { myCatalog } from './catalog';

export const registry = defineRegistry(myCatalog, {
  components: {
    // Inject the emit function alongside component props
    StreamButton: ({ label, streamId, emit }) => {
      return (
        <button onClick={() => emit('press', { streamId })}>
          {label}
        </button>
      );
    }
  },
  actions: {
    // Define the runtime function that resolves the incoming action stream object
    press: async (params, { setState }) => {
      if (params?.streamId) {
        // Mutate dynamic state path values based on the event payload
        setState('/state/activeStream', params.streamId);
      }
    }
  }
});
Use code with caution.
3. Consume the Progressive Stream
Wrap your component tree in the <JSONUIProvider> container to feed the client-side stream processor. The framework uses the useUIStream hook to listen to the backend JSONL patch stream endpoint natively. 

GitHub
 +3
typescript
import { JSONUIProvider, Renderer, useUIStream } from '@json-render/react';
import { registry } from './registry';

export function EventStreamDashboard() {
  const { spec, isStreaming } = useUIStream({
    api: '/api/ui-stream-endpoint' // Backend returning progressive JSONL rows
  });

  return (
    <JSONUIProvider registry={registry}>
      <Renderer spec={spec} registry={registry} loading={isStreaming} />
    </JSONUIProvider>
  );
}
Use code with caution.
