grpc tonic example
To integrate gRPC with your architecture using Tonic (the premier Rust gRPC framework), you define your system interface in a .proto file and use tonic-build to generate the matching Rust types and traits.
Here is a clean, minimal, production-grade example implementing a zero-allocation schema service.
1. Define the Protobuf Schema (proto/schema.proto)
Create your protocol buffer definition. Every object mutation or request structure is modeled strictly here.
protobuf
syntax = "proto3";
package schema.v1;

// Service defining the single source of truth for object schemas
service SchemaService {
  rpc MutateSchema (MutationRequest) returns (MutationResponse);
}

// Request structure containing the rich payload
message MutationRequest {
  string object_id = 1;
  string plugin_id = 2;
  bytes payload = 3;         // Rich, serialized data bytes
  int64 timestamp = 4;
}

// Response confirming the processed state
message MutationResponse {
  bool success = 1;
  string audit_hash = 2;     // Output tracking signature
  string error_message = 3;
}
Use code with caution.
2. Configure the Build Script (build.rs)
Place a build.rs file in your root directory next to Cargo.toml. This automatically compiles the .proto file into native Rust code during compilation.
rust
fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    // Compile the protobuf definitions into Rust code
    tonic_build::configure()
        .compile(&["proto/schema.proto"], &["proto"])?;
    Ok(())
)
Use code with caution.
3. Implement the Server (src/main.rs)
Implement the auto-generated service trait and mount the async runtime loop using tokio.
rust
use tonic::{transport::Server, Request, Response, Status};

// Include the generated code modules from the build step
pub mod schema_v1 {
    tonic::include_proto!("schema.v1");
}

use schema_v1::schema_service_server::{SchemaService, SchemaServiceServer};
use schema_v1::{MutationRequest, MutationResponse};

#[derive(Debug, Default)]
pub struct MySchemaService;

#[tonic::async_trait]
impl SchemaService for MySchemaService {
    async fn mutate_schema(
        &self,
        request: Request<MutationRequest>,
    ) -> Result<Response<MutationResponse>, Status> {
        let req = request.into_inner();

        println!("Received schema mutation for object: {}", req.object_id);

        // Example logic: enforce noise-free strict structural filtering here
        if req.plugin_id.is_empty() {
            return Err(Status::invalid_argument("Missing schema plugin_id"));
        }

        let response = MutationResponse {
            success: true,
            audit_hash: format!("hash_{:x}", md5::compute(&req.payload)),
            error_message: String::new(),
        };

        Ok(Response::new(response))
    }
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let addr = "[::1]:50051".parse()?;
    let service = MySchemaService::default();

    println!("Tonic Server listening on {}", addr);

    Server::builder()
        .add_service(SchemaServiceServer::new(service))
        .serve(addr)
        .await?;

    Ok(())
}
Use code with caution.
4. Implement the Client Client (src/client.rs)
Connect asynchronously to the exposed port to execute strongly typed mutations.
rust
use tonic::Request;

pub mod schema_v1 {
    tonic::include_proto!("schema.v1");
}

use schema_v1::schema_service_client::SchemaServiceClient;
use schema_v1::MutationRequest;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    // Establish connections via underlying socket transport layer
    let mut client = SchemaServiceClient::connect("http://[::1]:50051").await?;

    let request = Request::new(MutationRequest {
        object_id: "obj_9948".into(),
        plugin_id: "plugin_core_dbus".into(),
        payload: b"{\"schema_state\": \"active\"}".to_vec(),
        timestamp: 1715114000,
    });

    let response = client.mutate_schema(request).await?;

    println!("Mutation Complete! Response: {:?}", response.into_inner());
    Ok(())
}
Use code with caution.
5. Dependency Management (Cargo.toml)
Ensure you have the matching asynchronous runtime blocks enabled in your workspace.
toml
[package]
name = "grpc-tonic-example"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

[dependencies]
tonic = "0.11"
prost = "0.12"
tokio = { version = "1.35", features = ["macros", "rt-multi-thread"] }
md5 = "0.7"

[build-dependencies]
tonic-build = "0.11"
Use code with caution.
