To enable gRPC Server Reflection in your Tonic application, you need to compile a compiled binary representation of your protocol buffers (a FileDescriptorSet) during your build phase, and then expose it via the tonic-reflection service crate. This allows external CLI debugging tools like grpcurl or Postman to discover services dynamically without manually providing local .proto schema files. 

GitHub
 +3
Here is how to modify the previous Tonic example to natively serve schema reflection paths.
1. Update Build Settings to Generate Descriptor Sets (build.rs)
You must configure tonic_build to compile a .bin file containing the descriptor layout. This file is later read at server startup. 
rust
use std::env;
use std::path::PathBuf;

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    // Determine the build out directory path
    let out_dir = PathBuf::from(env::var("OUT_DIR").unwrap());

    // Compile proto definitions and save the binary schema descriptor set
    tonic_build::configure()
        .file_descriptor_set_path(out_dir.join("schema_descriptor.bin"))
        .compile(&["proto/schema.proto"], &["proto"])?;

    Ok(())
}
Use code with caution.
2. Update Server to Mount Reflection Services (src/main.rs)
Include the standard reflection builders into your Tokio server initialization loop. The binary artifact generated by build.rs is embedded safely at compile time via the include_bytes! macro.
rust
use tonic::{transport::Server, Request, Response, Status};

pub mod schema_v1 {
    tonic::include_proto!("schema.v1");

    // 1. Reference the embedded binary descriptor set array created in build.rs
    pub const FILE_DESCRIPTOR_SET: &[u8] =
        tonic::include_bytes!(concat!(env!("OUT_DIR"), "/schema_descriptor.bin"));
}

use schema_v1::schema_service_server::{SchemaService, SchemaServiceServer};
use schema_v1::{MutationRequest, MutationResponse};

#[derive(Debug, Default)]
pub struct MySchemaService;

#[tonic::async_trait]
impl SchemaService for MySchemaService {
    async fn mutate_schema(
        &self,
        _request: Request<MutationRequest>,
    ) -> Result<Response<MutationResponse>, Status> {
        Ok(Response::new(MutationResponse {
            success: true,
            audit_hash: "reflected_hash_stub".into(),
            error_message: String::new(),
        }))
    }
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let addr = "[::1]:50051".parse()?;
    let service = MySchemaService::default();

    // 2. Build the reflection service by mapping your proto package signature
    let reflection_service = tonic_reflection::server::Builder::configure()
        .register_encoded_file_descriptor_set(schema_v1::FILE_DESCRIPTOR_SET)
        .build_v1alpha()? // Exposes standard v1alpha discovery specs
        // If your crate/tools require v1 reflection endpoints, append .build_v1() here
        ;

    println!("Tonic Server running with Reflection on {}", addr);

    Server::builder()
        // Mount your standard logic implementations alongside reflection route nodes
        .add_service(SchemaServiceServer::new(service))
        .add_service(reflection_service)
        .serve(addr)
        .await?;

    Ok(())
}
Use code with caution.
3. Add Necessary Workspace Dependencies (Cargo.toml)
Ensure you import the core reflection library. 

GitHub
toml
[package]
name = "grpc-tonic-reflection-example"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

[dependencies]
tonic = "0.11"
tonic-reflection = "0.11" # Must match your major Tonic version flag
prost = "0.12"
tokio = { version = "1.35", features = ["macros", "rt-multi-thread"] }

[build-dependencies]
tonic-build = "0.11"
Use code with caution.
4. Testing Your Reflected Schema Endpoint
Once your server is running with cargo run, you can test dynamic runtime query introspection from a terminal without providing proto files locally:
List available services:
bash
grpcurl -plaintext localhost:50051 list
Use code with caution.
Output:
grpc.reflection.v1alpha.ServerReflection
schema.v1.SchemaService
Describe a specific schema method structurally:
bash
grpcurl -plaintext localhost:50051 describe schema.v1.SchemaService.MutateSchema
Use code with caution.
