To render the chat interface from the antigravity catalog entry in Vercel’s json-render, you need to ensure the catalog schema supports the conversational components and that your registry maps them to their respective UI layouts. 

json-render
Since json-render can mix full generative UI specs with inline conversation modes, rendering a chat stream requires binding an active data pathway to the component. 

json-render
1. Ensure antigravity Chat Components Exist in the Catalog
First, verify that your catalog definition (catalog.ts) explicitly exposes the chat container and stream payload schemas. 

json-render
typescript
import { defineCatalog } from '@json-render/core';
import { z } from 'zod';

export const antigravityCatalog = defineCatalog({
  components: {
    // The main wrapper matching your antigravity entry
    AntigravityChatContainer: {
      description: 'The host frame for the active agent chat stream',
      props: {
        streamId: z.string(),
        title: z.string().optional()
      }
    },
    // The entry structure itself used to loop over historical tokens
    ChatMessage: {
      description: 'Individual text or UI nodes within the chat thread',
      props: {
        role: z.enum(['user', 'agent', 'system']),
        content: z.string()
      }
    }
  }
});
Use code with caution.
2. Map the Components in the React Registry
Your registry binds the structural keys to real React components. To make it dynamic, link the ChatMessage loops to your json-render local state reference using the framework's data-binding convention ($state). 

json-render
typescript
import { defineRegistry } from '@json-render/react';
import { antigravityCatalog } from './catalog';

export const registry = defineRegistry(antigravityCatalog, {
  components: {
    AntigravityChatContainer: ({ streamId, title, children }) => {
      return (
        <div className="flex flex-col h-[500px] border border-zinc-800 rounded-lg bg-zinc-950">
          <div className="p-3 border-b border-zinc-800 font-semibold text-white">
            {title || 'Antigravity Session'} ({streamId})
          </div>
          {/* Scrollable container displaying the streamed elements */}
          <div className="flex-1 overflow-y-auto p-4 space-y-3">
            {children}
          </div>
        </div>
      );
    },
    ChatMessage: ({ role, content }) => {
      const isAgent = role === 'agent';
      return (
        <div className={`p-3 rounded-lg max-w-[80%] ${
          isAgent ? 'bg-blue-600 text-white mr-auto' : 'bg-zinc-800 text-zinc-100 ml-auto'
        }`}>
          <p className="text-sm font-medium opacity-70 mb-1">
            {isAgent ? 'Antigravity Agent' : 'User'}
          </p>
          <div className="text-sm whitespace-pre-wrap">{content}</div>
        </div>
      );
    }
  }
});
Use code with caution.
3. Supply the JSON UI Spec to the Renderer
To render the chat interface inline, the incoming JSON payload (or progressive server stream) needs to look like this. It utilizes json-render's structural list iteration ($repeat) bound to the message collection state pathway: 

json-render
 +1
json
{
  "type": "AntigravityChatContainer",
  "props": {
    "streamId": "stream_antigravity_01",
    "title": "Mission Control Room"
  },
  "children": [
    {
      "type": "ChatMessage",
      "$repeat": "$state/streams/stream_antigravity_01/messages",
      "props": {
        "role": "$item.role",
        "content": "$item.content"
      }
    }
  ]
}
Use code with caution.
4. Provide Runtime Framework Context
Finally, use the core <JSONUIProvider> and <Renderer> blocks to consume the JSON spec generated above. Ensure you feed the dynamic initialState mapping so the data indices ($state/streams/...) resolve cleanly. 

json-render
